# Overlay: build an image that runs entirely from RAM and writes nothing to
# flash. Use it with the flash profile, not instead of it:
#
#   idf.py -B build-ram -DSDKCONFIG=build-ram/sdkconfig -DSDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS="sdkconfig.defaults;sdkconfig.ramprobe" build
#
# `-DSDKCONFIG=` is not optional and the reason is a trap worth naming: idf.py
# writes `sdkconfig` in the *project* directory, not the build directory, so a
# second build directory silently reuses the first one's configuration and
# ignores SDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS entirely. The symptom is a `build-ram/attadipa.bin`
# that is byte-identical to the flash image and boots from flash — a RAM build
# that is not one, reported as success. Give each variant its own sdkconfig.
#
# This is the route that is *proved* to work on this unit, and it is the one to
# prefer for anything experimental, because there is nothing to undo afterwards.
# The flash route to a second app partition is closed on this board — the free
# `ota_1` slot sits at 0x1000000 and cannot be booted at all.
# WAVESHARE_RUNNING_OUR_CODE.md §1 and §2.
#
# Loading it: tools/flash/ramhold.py, and read its header before reaching for
# `esptool load-ram` instead. The CLI tool kills the image it just loaded, a few
# milliseconds after reporting success, by closing the port.
# Two symbols, and the second one is not optional: PURE_RAM_APP is a sub-option
# of the RAM build type (`depends on APP_BUILD_TYPE_RAM` in ESP-IDF's own
# Kconfig), so setting it alone is dropped in silence and the build comes out as
# an ordinary flash app that reports success. That is what happened here the
# first time: build-ram/sdkconfig came back with CONFIG_APP_BUILD_TYPE_APP_2NDBOOT=y
# and the CI guard that greps for the symbol is what would have caught it.
CONFIG_APP_BUILD_TYPE_RAM=y
CONFIG_APP_BUILD_TYPE_PURE_RAM_APP=y

# Keep the debug endpoint and its full-frame PSRAM snapshot out of the minimal
# RAM probe. The normal flash build enables it explicitly.
CONFIG_ATTADIPA_WATCH_CONTROL=n

# A RAM app has no partition table to read, and asking for one is a boot-time
# failure rather than an empty answer.
CONFIG_PARTITION_TABLE_CUSTOM=n
CONFIG_PARTITION_TABLE_SINGLE_APP=y
